# Roleplay kit: tell → show → tell, objection cards, rehearsal drill

**App:** {{APP_URL}} · **Deck:** `deck/northbridge-scam-shield-deck.pdf` · **Backup evidence:** `evidence/` (if anything breaks live)

## The two personas you will face
| | **Business stakeholder**: Priya Shah, Head of Fraud Operations (CRO in the room) | **Technical stakeholder**: Marcus Reid, Head of Fraud Engineering / Enterprise Architect |
|---|---|---|
| Cares about | Reimbursement £ on the P&L, Consumer Duty, team capacity, complaints/FOS, 4-hour SLA | Latency, skew, model risk (PRA SS1/23), PII, ops burden, lock-in, scale |
| Wins if you say | "same team, {{MODEL_VR}} of scam £ stopped, {{FP_RULES}} → {{FP_MODEL}} genuine holds per scam" | "one governed platform, Postgres semantics, explicit model contract, every decision audited" |
| Loses interest if you | lead with architecture, hide assumptions | hand-wave feature parity, latency or PII |

## Open with discovery (30 seconds; shows you listen before you pitch)
1. "How many payments does your team hold per day today, and what share turn out to be scams?"
2. "Where does PSR reimbursement land: Fraud's P&L or Finance's? Who owns the number?"
3. "What does an analyst do in the first five minutes of a held payment?"

## 60-second version (if time is cut)
> "Since October 2024 every APP-scam pound that gets past us comes back as a reimbursement claim, and half of it is our cost. With the **same number of payment holds**, our model catches **{{MODEL_VR}}** of scam value where today's rules catch **{{RULES_VR}}**, and it holds {{FP_MODEL}} genuine customers per scam instead of {{FP_RULES}}. Every hold arrives with a GenAI case note and a Consumer-Duty-safe call script. For Northbridge that's about **{{NET_VALUE}} a year**: **{{SENS_LOW}}** even on conservative assumptions. It runs on one governed Databricks platform, and we'd prove it on your data in a 6-week shadow pilot."

